## 2. Design Tokens and @theme

### Token Definition with `@theme`

Design tokens live in a `@theme` block in your entry CSS file. They become both `:root` CSS custom properties AND generated utility classes automatically.

```css
@theme {
  --color-brand: oklch(0.62 0.19 256);
  --font-display: "Cal Sans", "Inter", sans-serif;
  --radius-card: 0.75rem;
  --shadow-card: 0 1px 3px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.1);
  --breakpoint-3xl: 1920px;
}
```

The namespace prefix determines the generated utility:
- `--color-*` → `bg-brand`, `text-brand`, `border-brand`
- `--font-*` → `font-display`
- `--spacing-*` → padding, margin, gap, size utilities
- `--breakpoint-*` → responsive prefixes (`3xl:`)
- `--radius-*` → `rounded-card`
- `--shadow-*` → `shadow-card`

### `@theme` vs `@theme inline`

- **`@theme`** (default) — generates a CSS variable that participates in the cascade. Parent selectors CAN override it. **This is what you want for dark mode.**
- **`@theme inline`** — bakes the resolved value into the utility at build time. The variable cascade is lost. **Dark mode BREAKS with `@theme inline` for color tokens.**

Use `@theme inline` ONLY for computed values that must be resolved at build time (e.g., calc expressions for radius). Use plain `@theme` for anything that needs runtime switching.

### Color Space: Use OKLCH

Define colors in `oklch(lightness chroma hue)` instead of hex, rgb, or hsl. OKLCH is perceptually uniform — stepping lightness by a fixed amount produces visually consistent results across all hues.

```css
:root {
  --background: oklch(1 0 0);
  --foreground: oklch(0.145 0 0);
  --primary: oklch(0.205 0 0);
  --primary-foreground: oklch(0.985 0 0);
  --destructive: oklch(0.577 0.245 27.325);
  --muted: oklch(0.97 0 0);
  --muted-foreground: oklch(0.556 0 0);
  --border: oklch(0.922 0 0);
}
```

Keep color scales short — 3-5 steps per hue, not a full 50-950 scale. Every `@theme` token generates utilities across bg/text/border/ring/outline families.

