## 8. Component Architecture

### File Organization

```
components/
├── ui/               # shadcn primitives (registry-owned, do not edit)
│   ├── button.tsx
│   ├── card.tsx
│   ├── dialog.tsx
│   └── ...
├── forms/            # Form-specific composed components
│   ├── login-form.tsx
│   └── settings-form.tsx
├── layouts/          # Layout compositions
│   ├── app-sidebar.tsx
│   └── page-header.tsx
└── features/         # Feature-specific compositions
    ├── dashboard-card.tsx
    └── user-avatar.tsx
```

### Component Conventions

```tsx
// Named exports only, no default exports
export function DashboardCard({ title, children, className }: DashboardCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card className={cn("shadow-sm", className)}>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{title}</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>{children}</CardContent>
    </Card>
  )
}
```

- Functional components with explicit return types when non-trivial
- Props interface named `{ComponentName}Props`
- ALWAYS accept and spread `className` for composability
- Server Components by default; add `"use client"` only when interactivity is required
- Keep components under 150 lines — extract sub-components when exceeding
- No `React.forwardRef` in React 19 — `ref` is a regular prop

### State Management Patterns

- Server Components by default — move data fetching to the server
- `"use client"` only for: event handlers, browser APIs, useState/useEffect, third-party client libs
- Zustand for shared client state (not Redux, not Context API for complex state)
- TanStack Query (useQuery/useMutation) for server state — never raw fetch in client components
- React Hook Form + Zod for form state and validation

